"Bijective proofs of several conjectures on Jacobi permutations | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2608.11771)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; While the bijective proofs provide structural insight, the authors also provide an analytical solution to an open problem regarding the \"André–Entringer distribution\" on Jacobi permutations. ... These results not only deepen our understanding of Jacobi permutations but also demonstrate the power of tree-based bijections in modern enumerative combinatorics.\n\n## Analytic Approach: Trivariate Generating Functions\n\nWhile the bijective proofs provide structural insight, the authors also provide an analytical solution to an open problem regarding the \"André–Entringer distribution\" on Jacobi permutations. They seek a closed-form formula for the trivariate exponential generating function $F(t; x, y)$F(t;x,y).\n\nThis function is defined to track three properties: the number of ascents ($t$t), the number of elements smaller than the last letter ($x$x), and the number of elements larger than the last letter ($y$y):\n\n$$F(t; x, y) = \\sum_{n \\geq 1} \\sum_{\\pi \\in J_n} t^{asc(\\pi)} \\frac{x^{L(\\pi)} y^{R(\\pi)}}{n!}$$F(t;x,y)=n≥1∑​π∈J n​∑​t a sc(π)n!x L(π)y R(π)​\n\nwhere $L(\\pi) = last(\\pi)-1$L(π)=l a s t(π)−1 and $R(\\pi) = n - last(\\pi)$R(π)=n−l a s t(π). Using a recurrence relation for the number of Jacobi permutations $J_{n,k}(t)$J n,k​(t) with $asc=t$a sc=t and $last=k$l a s t=k:\n\n$$J_{n,k}(t) = \\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} \\binom{k-1}{j} J_{j,j}(t) J_{n-j-1,k-j-1}(t)$$J n,k​(t)=j=1∑k−1​(j k−1​)J j,j​(t)J n−j−1,k−j−1​(t)\n\nthe authors derive a system of differential equations. By solving these, they obtain the following elegant closed-form expression:\n\n$$F(t; x, y) = \\frac{(1 + \\rho)e^{\\rho y} + (\\rho - 1)e^{\\rho x}}{(1 + \\rho) + (\\rho - 1)e^{\\rho(x+y)}}$$F(t;x,y)=(1+ρ)+(ρ−1)e ρ(x+y)(1+ρ)e ρ y+(ρ−1)e ρ x​\n\nwhere the variable $\\rho$ρ is defined as:\n\n$$\\rho = \\sqrt{1 - 2t}$$ρ=1−2 t​\n\nThis formula is a powerful tool. It allows researchers to extract specific counts for permutations of any length $n$n with any number of ascents and any specific last letter just by looking at the coefficients of the Taylor expansion of $F$F.\n\n## Conclusion and Significance\n\nThe work by Lin, Wen, and Yan provides a comprehensive resolution to several standing conjectures in the study of Jacobi permutations. By successfully linking Jacobi permutations to André and simsun permutations through sophisticated bijections, the authors have unified various refined enumerations under the \"André–Entringer\" umbrella.\n\nThe significance of this research lies in its dual approach. The bijective proofs reveal the \"why\"—showing the explicit structural transformations that make these sets equivalent. Meanwhile, the generating function results provide the \"how\"—offering a precise mathematical formula to calculate these distributions. These results not only deepen our understanding of Jacobi permutations but also demonstrate the power of tree-based bijections in modern enumerative combinatorics. The techniques developed here, particularly the recursive tree transformations, are likely to be applicable to other classes of permutations and combinatorial objects in the future.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCours M2 (https://cours.ip2i.in2p3.fr/richard/CoursM2-1213.pdf)\nciteturn5search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.9 years ago; Coordonnées de Jacobi . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... Comportement vis-à-vis des permutations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... 9.4\n21\n5.2\nLimite rigoureuse sur la fenêtre borroméenne\n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n22\n6\nRésolution du problème à trois corps\n25\n6.1\nCoordonnées de Jacobi . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n25\n6.2\nComp